    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99313",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.407Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-18T08:42:06.681Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-20T05:11:35.305Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-20T05:11:35.305Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nHID: logitech-hidpp: fix race condition when accessing stale stack pointer\n\nThe driver uses hidpp->send_receive_buf to point to a stack-allocated\nbuffer in the synchronous command path (__do_hidpp_send_message_sync).\nHowever, this pointer is not cleared when the function returns.\n\nIf an event is processed (e.g. by a different thread) while the\nsend_mutex is held by a new command, but before that command has\nupdated send_receive_buf, the handler (hidpp_raw_hidpp_event) will\nobserve that the mutex is locked and dereference the stale pointer.\n\nThis results in an out-of-bounds access on a different thread's kernel\nstack (or a NULL pointer dereference on the very first command).\n\nFix this by:\n1. Clearing hidpp->send_receive_buf to NULL before releasing the mutex\n   in the synchronous command path.\n2. Moving the assignment of the local 'question' and 'answer' pointers\n   inside the mutex_is_locked() block in the handler, and adding\n   a NULL check before dereferencing."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 8.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:A - The triggering HID++ input report (id 0x10, 0x11, or 0x12) arrives on the Bluetooth HIDP interrupt channel: hidp_session_run() dequeues the L2CAP skb, hidp_recv_intr_frame() calls hidp_process_report(), hid_input_report() calls hidpp_raw_event(), and hidpp_raw_hidpp_event() dereferences the stale send_receive_buf. HID_BLUETOOTH_DEVICE() entries bind this driver to that radio-range transport.\nAC:L - The same peer creates both sides. A short report with rap.sub_id 0x41 makes hidpp_report_is_connect_event() schedule hidpp_connect_event(), and hidpp_root_get_protocol_version() then locks send_mutex in hidpp_send_message_sync() before __do_hidpp_send_message_sync() stores send_receive_buf, while the peer floods 0x10/0x11/0x12 reports into that window.\nPR:N - hidp_recv_intr_frame() and hidpp_raw_hidpp_event() accept the bonded peer's input report with no capable() check and no host credential. The attacker is the Bluetooth HID device matched by HID_BLUETOOTH_DEVICE(), not a local process.\nUI:N - On a bonded Logitech HID++ link, hidpp_probe() calls hid_device_io_start() and then hidpp_get_device_name(), and a peer-sent sub_id 0x41 report schedules hidpp_connect_event(). The peer sends those reports itself; no user open or mount is on the path.\nS:U - The *answer = *report write in hidpp_raw_hidpp_event() lands on the kernel stack of the thread that called __do_hidpp_send_message_sync(), which is the same host kernel authority as the HID++ driver.\nC:H - __do_hidpp_send_message_sync() leaves send_receive_buf aimed at the caller's stack hidpp_report. hidpp_raw_hidpp_event() reads question->fap.feature_index and funcindex_clientid through that expired pointer and, on a match, *answer = *report overwrites 64 bytes of the other thread's kernel stack.\nI:H - When hidpp_match_answer() or hidpp_match_error() matches the stale pointer, hidpp_raw_hidpp_event() executes *answer = *report and copies the peer-controlled 64-byte hidpp_report onto the previous command's stack slot left set by __do_hidpp_send_message_sync().\nA:H - send_receive_buf is NULL after the hidpp kzalloc, so the first command's hidpp_match_answer() NULL-dereferences and oopses. Later, the same path uses the expired stack pointer and faults or corrupts the command thread's stack."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
