    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:01:25.107Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv6: raw: fix use-after-free in rawv6_send_hdrinc()\n\nrawv6_send_hdrinc() caches the route before handing the skb to\nl3mdev_ip6_out(). The VRF output path may replace skb->dst and release\nthe original route, leaving the cached pointer stale.\n\nThe function later dereferences that pointer for statistics accounting\nand as the output device passed to NF_HOOK(). Its RCU read-side critical\nsection starts after l3mdev_ip6_out(), so it cannot prevent the old route\nfrom being reclaimed in between.\n\nReload the route from the current skb destination after\nl3mdev_ip6_out() returns.\n\nKASAN reports a slab-use-after-free in rawv6_sendmsg(), with the route\nallocated by ip6_dst_alloc(), freed by dst_destroy() from rcu_core(), and\nthe related RCU callback queued from vrf_l3_out()."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - sendmsg() on an AF_INET6 SOCK_RAW socket enters rawv6_send_hdrinc() through inet_sendmsg() and rawv6_sendmsg() when HDRINCL is set. The route that vrf_ip6_out_redirect() drops with skb_dst_drop() is the one this local send cached before calling l3mdev_ip6_out().\nAC:H - HDRINCL sets FLOWI_FLAG_KNOWN_NH, so ip6_pol_route() returns a one-ref ip6_rt_cache_alloc() route when the nexthop has no gateway, and skb_dst_drop() only call_rcu_hurry()s dst_destroy. rawv6_send_hdrinc() then uses that rt with no attacker-controlled stall after l3mdev_ip6_out() returns, so the slab is reclaimed only if an RCU grace period finishes in that gap.\nPR:L - inet6_create() requires CAP_NET_RAW in the socket netns user namespace. vrf_ip6_out_redirect() runs only when the egress device is an l3 slave and qdisc_tx_is_default() is false, and creating that VRF and qdisc needs CAP_NET_ADMIN in rtnetlink_rcv_msg(). An unprivileged user gets both via a user and network namespace.\nUI:N - The attacker opens the AF_INET6 SOCK_RAW socket, adds the VRF qdisc, and calls sendmsg() to an on-link global address.\nS:U - The freed object is the rt6_info for this socket's own output route, and the corruption is kernel memory in the caller's security domain.\nC:H - After dst_destroy() frees the rt6_info, rawv6_send_hdrinc() loads rt->rt6i_idev for IP6_INC_STATS() and rt->dst.dev for NF_HOOK(). Reclaiming that ip6_dst_ops slab lets the attacker choose those pointers and disclose kernel memory through the resulting dereferences.\nI:H - IP6_INC_STATS() increments a counter through the freed rt->rt6i_idev, and NF_HOOK(NFPROTO_IPV6, NF_INET_LOCAL_OUT) uses the freed rt->dst.dev as the output net_device. Reusing the route slab supplies those pointers and yields a kernel write.\nA:H - dst_destroy() frees the route from rcu_core() after vrf_l3_out() queues the callback, and rawv6_send_hdrinc() then dereferences it. That slab use-after-free oopses the kernel; KASAN reports it in rawv6_sendmsg()."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
