    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99343",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.409Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:21:51.069Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:01:38.188Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:01:38.188Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: x86/mmu: Bug the VM if KVM attempts to walk more levels than the MMU has\n\nExtend the \"EFER.LMA && !CR4.PAE\" check, which exists largely to guard\nagainst KVM configuring a paging32 MMU with more than 2 levels of paging,\nwith a very explicit check for exactly that: that KVM isn't trying to walk\nmore levels of paging than the MMU template provides.  I.e. harden KVM\nagainst all bugs that would cause KVM to generates accesses beyond the\nbounds of guest_walker's arrays, regardless of how KVM ended up with the\nmisconfigured MMU.\n\nNote, don't use w->cpu_role.base.level directly as the paging64 template\nonly provides two levels of page tables for PAE paging on 32-bit hosts, and\nhandles the third level by manually emulating the PDPTR access."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.1,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - KVM_SET_NESTED_STATE in kvm_vcpu_ioctl calls svm_set_nested_state, which copies the L1 VMCB save area through svm_copy_vmrun_state into vmcb01. nested_svm_init_mmu_context passes that CR4 and EFER to kvm_init_shadow_npt_mmu, so the paging mode is chosen from ioctl payload.\nAC:L - The caller puts EFER.LMA=1, EFER.LME=0 and CR4.PAE=0 in that save area; nested_vmcb_check_save checks PAE only when LME and CR0.PG are both set. KVM_RUN then enters paging32_walk_addr_generic. Nested SVM and NPT both default on (svm.c nested=true, npt_enabled=true), and the caller supplies the VMCB, memslots and guest PTEs.\nPR:L - svm_set_nested_state runs from the KVM_SET_NESTED_STATE vCPU ioctl with no capability check. A process that can open /dev/kvm, create a VM, and set EFER.SVME in guest CPUID can issue it.\nUI:N - That same process writes the nested state and calls KVM_RUN, and paging32_walk_addr_generic runs on the vCPU it created.\nS:U - Only svm_copy_vmrun_state writes the illegal L1 EFER/CR4 into vmcb01. nested_svm_vmrun copies vcpu->arch.efer and vcpu->arch.cr4, which set_efer and kvm_set_cr4 keep consistent, so the bad role is a host-ioctl state. The overflow is in the host stack frame of paging32_walk_addr_generic.\nC:N - gpte_changed and update_accessed_dirty_bits reread out-of-range walker slots that alias prefetch_ptes, ptep_user and pt_access inside the same guest_walker, which hold the caller's PTE bytes and memslot HVA. walker.fault and the GPA returned by paging32_gva_to_gpa are filled from the faulting address and the leaf PTE.\nI:H - paging32_walk_addr_generic writes table_gfn, pte_gpa, ptep_user, ptes and pt_access at index cpu_role.base.level-1 (3 or 4) past PT_MAX_FULL_LEVELS (2). FNAME(fetch) passes the aliased table_gfn to kvm_mmu_get_shadow_page, which allocates a shadow page and write-tracks that attacker-chosen GFN.\nA:H - account_shadowed calls __kvm_write_track_add_gfn with __gfn_to_memslot's result, and update_gfn_write_track dereferences slot->arch.gfn_write_track with no NULL check, so a GFN in no memslot oopses the host. A gpte_changed mismatch instead returns RET_PF_RETRY and spins the vCPU thread."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
