            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:01:39.281Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: nSVM: Ignore EFER.LMA if EFER.LME=0 when preparing L2 state\n\nForce EFER.LMA=0 if EFER.LME=0 when preparing L2 state for VMRUN, i.e.\nmimic real hardware's behavior of ignoring EFER.LMA if EFER.LME=0.  VMRUN\nunfortunately allows the nonsensical combination, i.e. doesn't fail, but\nKVM itself has an invariant EFER.LMA can be set et if and only if EFER.LME\nis set.   Breaking that invariant can lead to a variety of issue,\nparticularly in MMU code that keys off EFER.LMA when determining whether to\nemulate/virtualization 4/5-level paging versus PAE paging."
                }
            ],
