    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:01:50.749Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nSUNRPC: fix oversized GSS proxy token copy\n\ngss_read_proxy_verf() allocates one page per token chunk, but it copies the\nentire linear head of an RPC request into in_token->pages[0]. A UDP datagram\ncan leave more than PAGE_SIZE bytes in the linear head, so a large\nRPCSEC_GSS INIT token overwrites following pages.\n\nCopy the linear head in page-sized chunks before switching to the request\npage array. The linear head itself can span multiple pages."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 8.1,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - svc_udp_recvfrom() accepts a UDP RPC Call and, for a linear skb, sets rq_arg.head to skb->data. svc_authenticate() then calls svcauth_gss_accept(), which on RPC_GSS_PROC_INIT calls svcauth_gss_proxy_init() and gss_read_proxy_verf(). The memcpy there copies the remaining linear head, the attacker-supplied rpc_gss_init_arg token. UDP is routable; netvsc_alloc_recv_skb() and macb_rx_frame() build that linear skb from a received frame.\nAC:H - The copy in gss_read_proxy_verf() exceeds the alloc_page() only when svc_udp_recvfrom() has placed a linear skb longer than PAGE_SIZE in rq_arg.head. Page-frag receives and IP reassembly leave the XDR head at the PAGE_SIZE buffer from svc_alloc_arg(), which csum_partial_copy_to_xdr() fills. Triggering the overflow depends on a pre-existing receive path that allocated one linear buffer bigger than a page, which the sender does not control.\nPR:N - svcauth_gss_accept() handles RPC_GSS_PROC_INIT before gss_svc_searchbyctx(). svcauth_gss_proc_init() checks only that the verifier is AUTH_NULL with length 0 and, when use_gss_proxy() is set, calls svcauth_gss_proxy_init(). The copy in gss_read_proxy_verf() runs before gssp_accept_sec_context_upcall(), so no context or uid is verified.\nUI:N - The attacker sends the UDP RPC Call that contains the oversized gss_token. Nothing on the path from svc_udp_recvfrom() to gss_read_proxy_verf() waits for a mount, an open, or any other action by a victim.\nS:U - The bytes past pages[0] are host kernel memory in the sunrpc worker running svcauth_gss_proxy_init(), so the corruption stays inside the kernel that received the Call.\nC:H - The memcpy writes attacker-chosen token bytes past page_address(in_token->pages[0]) into the next direct-map page for the full linear-head length. Overwriting a neighboring allocation's pointer or length yields a disclosure primitive.\nI:H - gss_read_proxy_verf() allocates one order-0 page per token chunk, then memcpy's the entire linear head into pages[0]. The attacker chooses both the bytes and the length through the rpc_gss_init_arg opaque token, so the overflow is a write into adjacent kernel memory.\nA:H - That out-of-bounds write runs in the nfsd/sunrpc thread and either faults on the next page or corrupts live kernel data. Either result oopses or panics the host, and a new PROC_INIT datagram repeats it."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
