    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:14.076Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsmb: client: don't resurrect an exiting tcon from cached dir workers\n\ncfids_laundromat_worker() bumps tcon->tc_count for every expired open\ncfid and queues cached_dir_offload_close() without checking whether the\ntcon is already being torn down.  Racing with the final cifs_put_tcon(),\nthe tcon comes back from zero and gets torn down twice:\n\n  T1                                  T2\n  cifs_put_tcon()\n   --tc_count == 0, TID_EXITING\n   tconInfoFree()\n    free_cached_dirs()\n     cancel_delayed_work_sync()       cfids_laundromat_worker()\n                                       ++tc_count == 1\n                                       queue_work(serverclose_wq)\n    kfree(tcon->origin_fullpath)\n    kfree(tcon)\n                                      cached_dir_offload_close()\n                                       close_cached_dir()  [UAF]\n                                       cifs_put_tcon()\n                                        tconInfoFree()     [double free]\n\ncached_dir_lease_break() also bumps tc_count without tc_lock, racing with\nthe laundromat's locked update.\n\nTake the tcon reference through a helper that refuses once the tcon is\nTID_EXITING or its refcount has dropped to zero.  Queued put_work and\nclose_work then always hold a tcon reference, so tconInfoFree() can't\nrun before they finish and free_cached_dirs() doesn't need to flush any\nworkqueue.  If the laundromat can't get a reference, put the cfid back on\nthe dying list for free_cached_dirs() to free; its handle goes away with\nthe tree connection.\n\nThe lease break path finds the tcon on ses->tcon_list under\ncifs_tcp_ses_lock, so it can't see TID_EXITING, but it now takes tc_lock."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.5,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - cifs_demultiplex_thread() reads an SMB2 lease break (SMB2_OPLOCK_BREAK, StructureSize 44) and calls smb2_is_valid_oplock_break() -> smb2_is_valid_lease_break() -> cached_dir_lease_break(tcon, rsp->LeaseKey). Pre-fix that function did ++tcon->tc_count with no tc_lock.\nAC:H - That unlocked ++ races cfids_laundromat_worker's tc_lock ++ only while a different cfid is expiring. The server can send the LeaseKey but cannot arm the dir_cache_timeout laundromat or force the two plain int RMWs to interleave, and the first matching break clears has_lease.\nPR:N - Any writer on the connected SMB socket can send that break: cifs_demultiplex_thread() calls is_oplock_break with no client credential check, and smb2_verify_signature() returns 0 immediately for SMB2_OPLOCK_BREAK.\nUI:R - Two live cfids must already exist. smb3_qfs_tcon() caches the root at mount via open_cached_dir(), and a second directory is cached from cifs_readdir() or lookup. The victim has to mount the share and touch another directory before a break can race the laundromat.\nS:U - The object that gets freed is struct cifs_tcon in the client kernel that holds the mount. A use-after-free there is kernel memory corruption in the same security domain as the cifs superblock.\nC:H - If one ++ is lost, tc_count is one short, so the second cifs_put_tcon() in cached_dir_offload_close() hits zero and tconInfoFree() kfree()s the tcon under a still-mounted superblock. Reusing that kmalloc slot exposes the old tcon and whatever replaces it.\nI:H - close_cached_dir() runs smb2_close_cached_fid() -> SMB2_close() on cfid->tcon after that free, and tconInfoFree() kfree()s cfids while the other worker still locks cfid->cfids. Reclaiming the slab is a kernel write primitive.\nA:H - The premature tconInfoFree() path calls free_cached_dirs() and kfree(tcon) while cached_dir_offload_close() still dereferences cfid->tcon and cfid->cfids->cfid_list_lock, which oopses the client kernel."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
