{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99388",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.412Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:23:27.698Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:16.232Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:16.232Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsmb: smbdirect: fix buffer length underflow in RDMA transfers\n\nWhen a remote buffer descriptor is larger than the remaining local\nbuffer, smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit() clamps its length and sets\nbuf_len to zero. The common accounting code then subtracts the clamped\nlength from buf_len, causing it to underflow. If another descriptor\nfollows, the loop processes it with a wrapped remaining length and\nincludes memory beyond the local buffer in the RDMA transfer.\n\nA client can trigger an out-of-bounds kernel write with an SMB WRITE\nrequest, or an out-of-bounds kernel read with an SMB READ request, by\nsupplying multiple RDMA buffer descriptors with an oversized descriptor\nbefore the last one.\n\nRemove the premature assignment to buf_len. Subtracting the clamped length\nalready consumes the remaining buffer, so the next iteration stops and\nonly descriptors within the local buffer are used to build work requests."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 8.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - A remote SMB Direct client puts the bad descriptor lengths in the SMB2 READ ReadChannelInfo or SMB2 WRITE WriteChannelInfo of the PDU that ksmbd_conn_handler_loop reads via smb_direct_read; smb2_read/smb2_write then call smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit. That listener binds AF_INET port 5445 for iWARP (TCP) and port 445 for RoCEv2, both routable IP.\nAC:L - The client chooses the descriptor lengths, and check_rdma_desc only requires their sum to cover Length or RemainingBytes, so an oversized descriptor followed by another one makes buf_len wrap in smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit. The client opens the SMB Direct connection and the file itself; a modest overflow fits the connection's existing RDMA credit pool.\nPR:L - smb2_check_user_session drops SMB2 READ and WRITE that lack a valid session, and smb2_read/smb2_write then require a tree connect and an open file with FILE_READ_DATA or FILE_WRITE_DATA. That is an authenticated share user who already finished session setup before the descriptor loop runs.\nUI:N - The authenticated client alone opens the file and sends the SMB2 READ or WRITE whose ReadChannelInfo or WriteChannelInfo carries the descriptors.\nS:U - The HCA DMA stays inside the host kernel that built the SG list from the ksmbd buffer in smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit, which is the same security authority as the corrupted memory.\nC:H - SMB2 READ calls smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit with is_read false, and the unclamped later descriptor makes smbdirect_connection_rdma_get_sg_list RDMA-write memory past aux_payload_buf to the client rkey. The client-chosen descriptor length covers many pages of adjacent kernel memory.\nI:H - SMB2 WRITE calls smbdirect_connection_rdma_xmit with is_read true (DMA_FROM_DEVICE), so the same later descriptor RDMA-reads bytes from the client's registered buffer into kernel memory past the kvzalloc buffer in smb2_write_rdma. Those bytes are chosen by the client.\nA:H - That RDMA read writes attacker-controlled data over adjacent kernel heap after the kvzalloc/kvmalloc buffer, which is enough to oops the host. A length that runs off a vmalloc mapping also puts a NULL page from vmalloc_to_page into the SG list."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
                    "vendor": "Linux",
                    "defaultStatus": "unaffected",
                    "repo": "https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git",
