    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:17.329Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsmb: server: size directory name conversion buffer correctly\n\nA 2048-byte ASCII directory name needs 4098 bytes when converted to\nUTF-16, including the terminator. ksmbd_convert_dir_info_name() caps\nits allocation at PATH_MAX and writes past the end of that buffer.\n\nAllocate space for one UTF-16 code unit per input byte plus the\nterminator. Use kmalloc_array() to check the size calculation."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.5,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - ksmbd_convert_dir_info_name() is reached from smb2_query_dir() through __query_dir() and smb2_populate_readdir_entry(). The bytes it converts are the directory entry the client previously sent as the SMB2 CREATE Name, via smb2_get_name() and smb2_creat()/ksmbd_vfs_create(), not a pre-existing on-disk structure.\nAC:H - The kmalloc is min(4*name_len, PATH_MAX), so smb_strtoUTF16() writes past it only when name_len>=2048 (2*name_len+2>4096). ext4_lookup() returns -ENAMETOOLONG above EXT4_NAME_LEN (255). The attacker cannot switch the share to a FUSE mount with fc->name_max set to FUSE_NAME_MAX.\nPR:L - smb2_check_user_session() and smb2_get_ksmbd_tcon() run before smb2_query_dir(), and planting the component requires smb2_creat() with write access on the share. That is an authenticated share user, not a pre-authentication packet and not CAP_SYS_ADMIN.\nUI:N - The same authenticated client sends the SMB2 CREATE that stores the long component and the SMB2 QUERY_DIRECTORY that converts it. No other user has to mount media or open a file.\nS:U - The out-of-bounds store is in the ksmbd worker's kernel heap and yields host kernel privilege. It does not cross a guest-to-host or IOMMU boundary.\nC:H - With a PATH_MAX-1 component, smb_strtoUTF16() writes 2*name_len+2 bytes (8192) into the 4096-byte buffer in ksmbd_convert_dir_info_name(). Corrupting adjacent slab objects is enough to build a kernel memory disclosure.\nI:H - The bytes written past that buffer are the UTF-16 form of the attacker-chosen SMB2 CREATE name, and the client picks the length. That heap out-of-bounds write can overwrite neighboring objects and control data.\nA:H - A 2048-byte ASCII name writes the terminating UTF-16 NUL two bytes past the PATH_MAX allocation and oopses the ksmbd thread. A longer accepted name smashes a full neighboring page."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
