{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99395",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.412Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:23:33.347Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:18.415Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:18.415Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nxfs: bound inode fork length against the fork size during log recovery\n\nxlog_recover_inode_commit_pass2() copies an inode log item's data/attr\nfork region into the inode buffer using the on-log region length without\nbounding it against the fork capacity, e.g.:\n\n\tlen = item->ri_buf[2].iov_len;\n\tmemcpy(XFS_DFORK_DPTR(dip), src, len);\n\nThe only guard is an ASSERT, which is a no-op on production kernels\n(CONFIG_XFS_DEBUG off), and xfs_dinode_verify() runs only after the copy.\nA crafted image with a dirty log can therefore drive a heap out-of-bounds\nwrite at mount time. The XFS_ILOG_DBROOT sibling already passes\nXFS_DFORK_DSIZE as a bound; the DDATA/DEXT and ADATA/AEXT memcpy paths\ndid not.\n\nBound each logged fork region against the destination fork size before\ncopying it, and reject the log item with -EFSCORRUPTED when it does not\nfit. Because the recovered inode is only verified after the fork data has\nbeen copied in, the checks are done up front, before any memcpy into the\non-disk inode."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The overflowing bytes are the on-disk log fork region: xlog_recover_add_to_trans records oh_len as ri_buf[].iov_len, and xlog_recover_inode_commit_pass2 copies that length into the inode buffer from xlog_recover pass 2, reached by xfs_log_mount in xfs_fs_fill_super. The log record is image data, not a protocol message.\nAC:L - The attacker sets a dirty log (tail_blk != head_blk), ilf_fields to XFS_ILOG_DDATA, XFS_ILOG_DEXT, XFS_ILOG_ADATA, or XFS_ILOG_AEXT, and an oh_len larger than XFS_DFORK_DSIZE or XFS_DFORK_ASIZE. xlog_recover then hits the memcpy on a normal mount; no race, media fault, or norecovery mount option is involved.\nPR:N - Supplying the image requires no credential. The mount and fsopen paths reach recovery only after mount_capable(), which demands init-namespace CAP_SYS_ADMIN because xfs_fs_type does not set FS_USERNS_MOUNT; that capability is used by the user who performs the mount.\nUI:R - xlog_recover_inode_commit_pass2 is called only from xfs_log_mount during xfs_mountfs in xfs_fs_fill_super. File operations on an already-mounted filesystem cannot inject the oversized log oh_len, so a user has to mount the supplied image.\nS:U - The write corrupts the host kernel's inode buffer from xfs_buf_alloc_backing_mem and memory adjacent to it. Impact stays in that kernel; XFS is not mountable from a user namespace, and the bug is not a guest-to-host or IOMMU crossing.\nC:H - The memcpy copies attacker-chosen fork bytes for an attacker-chosen iov_len with no production check against XFS_DFORK_DSIZE. A v5 image with a 1024-byte block and ilf_len of one block uses a 1024-byte kmalloc from xfs_buf_alloc_kmem, so a multi-kilobyte oh_len is a controlled heap overflow that can be leveraged to disclose adjacent memory.\nI:H - The DDATA/DEXT memcpy uses ri_buf iov_len with no comparison to XFS_DFORK_DSIZE; the nearby ASSERT only requires len == xlog_calc_iovec_len(ilf_dsize), and ilf_dsize is a uint16 the image sets. The ADATA/AEXT path's len <= XFS_DFORK_ASIZE check was an ASSERT, absent unless CONFIG_XFS_DEBUG is on.\nA:H - The memcpy runs before xfs_dinode_verify. Writing past the inode buffer corrupts neighboring kernel allocations and oopses or panics the host; returning -EFSCORRUPTED from recovery does not undo the overflow."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
                    "vendor": "Linux",
                    "defaultStatus": "unaffected",
                    "repo": "https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git",
