{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99400",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.412Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:23:37.667Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:21.700Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:21.700Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nxfs: reject out-of-range attribute value lengths in xfs_attr_copy_value\n\nxfs_attr_copy_value() takes the value length as a signed int and, for a\nremote xattr, is handed args->rmtvaluelen.  That field is filled from the\non-disk __be32 xfs_attr_leaf_name_remote.valuelen in\nxfs_attr3_leaf_getvalue(), so a crafted length such as 0x80000000 is\nstored into the signed rmtvaluelen as a negative number.\n\nThe \"buffer too small\" guard in xfs_attr_copy_value() is a signed\ncomparison:\n\n\tif (args->valuelen < valuelen)\n\t\treturn -ERANGE;\n\nA negative valuelen therefore compares as smaller than the caller's\nbuffer size, skips the -ERANGE path, and is then used as a copy length,\nleading to an out-of-bounds copy of a full remote block into a small\ngetxattr(2) buffer on a mounted crafted image.\n\nReject a value length that is negative or larger than the maximum xattr\nsize before it is used, so a bogus on-disk length can no longer slip\nthrough the value copier."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The overflowing length is the on-disk __be32 xfs_attr_leaf_name_remote.valuelen, stored into signed args->rmtvaluelen by xfs_attr3_leaf_getvalue. getxattr(2) only passes the destination buffer size, so the malformed input is the mounted image and the vector is local.\nAC:L - A crafted leaf value such as 0x80000000 is negative as a signed int, so args->valuelen < valuelen in xfs_attr_copy_value is false for any positive getxattr size. xfs_attr_rmtval_copyout then memcpy's one remote block; the attacker builds that image, with no race or hardware fault.\nPR:N - Reading the attr needs no credential: xattr_permission() returns 0 for security.* and only inode MAY_READ for user.*, and xfs_xattr_get checks neither capability. xfs_fs_type has no FS_USERNS_MOUNT, so the CAP_SYS_ADMIN mount is the victim's action.\nUI:R - xfs_attr_copy_value runs only after the image is mounted, from getxattr via do_getxattr, xfs_xattr_get, xfs_attr_node_get and xfs_attr3_leaf_getvalue. Mounting the attacker-supplied image is the required victim action.\nS:U - The out-of-bounds write hits the mounting kernel's heap, the kvzalloc buffer in do_getxattr. The corrupted security authority is that same kernel.\nC:H - xfs_attr_rmtval_copyout memcpy's attacker-chosen remote-block bytes, min of the unsigned rmtvaluelen and xfs_attr3_rmt_buf_space(), into a getxattr buffer that can be a single byte. That heap overflow can disclose kernel memory.\nI:H - The negative rmtvaluelen skips the ERANGE guard in xfs_attr_copy_value, and xfs_attr_rmtval_copyout then writes a full remote block past the kvzalloc'd getxattr buffer. That is an attacker-controlled out-of-bounds write.\nA:H - Smashing heap objects beside do_getxattr's buffer, while xfs_attr_rmtval_get continues because the length converted to unsigned stays far above zero, oopses or panics the kernel."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
                    "vendor": "Linux",
                    "defaultStatus": "unaffected",
                    "repo": "https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git",
