        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99441",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.415Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:41:25.953Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:54.443Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:54.443Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnvmet-auth: serialize access to DH-HMAC-CHAP state to fix UAF\n\nA REPLY AUTH_SEND command can derive a dhchap_skey key while a\nconcurrent AUTH_RECEIVE command observing this REPLY state executes\nthe failure path and frees that key. The REPLY command then continues\nand writes into freed memory, causing a use-after-free. KASAN reports\nit as:\n\n  BUG: KASAN: slab-use-after-free in __sha256_final\n  Write of size 4 at addr ffff88800359c540\n  ...\n  nvmet_auth_sq_free (drivers/nvme/target/auth.c:237)\n  nvmet_execute_auth_receive (drivers/nvme/target/fabrics-cmd-auth.c:621)\n\nAdd a mutex to each submission queue to serialize authentication\ncommands and timeout handling. Unlock the mutex before completing\nrequests and ignore outdated timeout callbacks. Limit timeout delays\nto prevent immediate expiry on 32-bit kernels."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 8.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:A - A Fibre Channel initiator triggers the race with two in-flight commands on one submission queue: an Authentication Send carrying a DH-HMAC-CHAP Reply, and an Authentication Receive. lpfc_nvmet_unsol_fcp_event delivers each FCP IU to nvmet_fc_rcv_fcp_req, which reaches nvmet_execute_auth_send and nvmet_execute_auth_receive; the attacker has to be on that FC SAN.\nAC:L - The attacker submits both commands. nvmet_auth_reply stays in nvmet_auth_ctrl_sesskey, with dhchap_step still NVME_AUTH_DHCHAP_MESSAGE_REPLY, while nvme_auth_gen_session_key blocks in crypto_wait_req on the DH shared secret. The receive runs nvmet_execute_auth_receive on lpfc's phba->wq or another CQ during that wait.\nPR:N - nvmet_parse_fabrics_admin_cmd dispatches Authentication Send and Authentication Receive without nvmet_check_auth_status. nvmet_auth_ctrl_sesskey allocates sq->dhchap_skey before nvmet_auth_reply's crypto_memneq checks the host response, so the DHCHAP secret is unverified; Connect only matches the self-asserted hostnqn in nvmet_host_allowed.\nUI:N - The initiator sends Connect, the DH-HMAC-CHAP Negotiate on Authentication Send, the Authentication Receive that sets dhchap_step to REPLY, and then the overlapping Reply and Receive on the same queue.\nS:U - The corrupted object is sq->dhchap_skey on the nvmet host's kernel heap, the same security authority as the target. This is a kernel memory corruption in that host.\nC:H - The receive failure path calls nvmet_auth_sq_free, which kfree's sq->dhchap_skey and sq->dhchap_c1 while nvmet_auth_reply still uses them. nvme_auth_hash writes the digest through the freed skey, and nvmet_auth_host_hash passes it to nvme_auth_augmented_challenge, so a reclaimed slab is read as key material.\nI:H - __sha256_final's put_unaligned_be32 stores the session-key digest into the buffer nvmet_auth_ctrl_sesskey allocated as sq->dhchap_skey after the concurrent receive has freed it. That write hits a reclaimed hash-sized kmalloc slot (32 bytes for the default SHA-256) and is a kernel heap write primitive.\nA:H - The digest store into the freed sq->dhchap_skey slab is a kernel use-after-free that oopses the target, and the receive's FAILURE1 arm also calls nvmet_ctrl_fatal_error, which sets NVME_CSTS_CFS and queues nvmet_fatal_error_handler to delete the controller."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
