    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:56.638Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nufs: use u64 for directory size in ufs_last_byte\n\nufs1_read_inode() and ufs2_read_inode() copy the untrusted 64-bit\non-disk size into inode->i_size.  ufs_last_byte() then truncates that\nvalue to an unsigned int before calculating the valid extent of a\ndirectory folio.\n\nA directory size of exactly 4 GiB is truncated to zero.  In\nufs_find_entry(), subtracting the requested record length from that zero\nforms an endpoint almost 4 GiB beyond the mapped folio.  A crafted UFS2\nimage produces:\n\n  BUG: KASAN: use-after-free in ufs_find_entry+0x583/0x6e0 [ufs]\n  Read of size 1\n  ...\n  ufs_find_entry\n  ufs_inode_by_name\n  ufs_lookup\n  __lookup_slow\n  path_lookupat\n\nThe KASAN classification reflects that the adjacent physical page was\nfree; the source-level operation is an out-of-folio read.  A controlled\nUFS1 test with CONFIG_UFS_FS_WRITE enabled also made ufs_find_entry()\nreturn a directory entry from an adjacent anonymous page.  unlink() then\npassed that pointer to ufs_delete_entry(), which cleared the adjacent\npage's 3\n2-bit d_ino.  This write result reproduced three out of three\ntimes.\n\nUFS normally requires a privileged mount path.  A relevant boundary is\na privileged automounter or image-processing service handling an\nattacker-supplied filesystem.  The write primitive additionally requires\nUFS1 to be mounted read-write with CONFIG_UFS_FS_WRITE enabled; the read\nis reachable with read-only UFS2.\n\nKeep the intermediate size arithmetic at 64 bits so non-final pages are\nbounded at PAGE_SIZE.  With this change, the same controlled tests reach\nthe real on-disk directory entry and produce no adjacent-page write or\nKASAN report.  A reproducer and full validation logs are available on\nrequest.\n\nThe vulnerable helper is present in v7.2-rc3, v7.1, v6.18.38, v6.12.95,\nv6.6.111, and upstream master at 1229e2e57a5c.  Runtime reproduction and\nfix validation were performed in an x86-64 QEMU guest running v7.2-rc3\nwith generic KASAN."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The on-disk directory ui_size is copied into inode->i_size by ufs1_read_inode() or ufs2_read_inode() from the inode block read with sb_bread(). ufs_last_byte() is reached from path_lookupat() through ufs_lookup(), ufs_inode_by_name() and ufs_find_entry(), or from getdents() through ufs_readdir().\nAC:L - A directory ui_size of 4294967296 makes ufs_last_byte() truncate inode->i_size to unsigned 0 on page 0, and ufs_find_entry() adds the unsigned wrap of 0 minus UFS_DIR_REC_LEN(namelen) to the folio address. The image supplies that ui_size and a check-passing directory block, so the first lookup takes this path.\nPR:N - The party who supplies the image has no host credentials. ufs_fs_type.fs_flags is FS_REQUIRES_DEV, so mount_capable() requires capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN) of the mounting process; ufs_lookup() then only applies may_lookup() to the ui_mode loaded by ufs1_read_inode().\nUI:R - ufs_fill_super() reaches ufs_iget(UFS_ROOTINO) and d_make_root(). The ufs_last_byte() underflow runs when a person mounts the crafted image and looks up a name or calls getdents on that directory.\nS:U - The folio overrun in ufs_find_entry() and the dir->d_ino store in ufs_delete_entry() both affect memory of the same kernel that mounted the volume.\nC:H - After the unsigned wrap, the ufs_find_entry() and ufs_readdir() end pointer lies almost 4GiB past the address from ufs_get_folio(). The loops parse the next physical page as ufs_dir_entry records, and ufs_readdir() passes de->d_name and ufs_get_de_namlen() to dir_emit() when d_ino is nonzero.\nI:H - ufs_unlink() passes the ufs_find_entry() pointer to ufs_delete_entry(), which stores 0 to dir->d_ino. With a 4GiB directory that pointer addresses the next physical page; the reported UFS1 run with CONFIG_UFS_FS_WRITE cleared that adjacent 32-bit d_ino on three of three tries.\nA:H - The read is the KASAN use-after-free in ufs_find_entry() reached from path_lookupat(). If the following dirent's d_reclen is 0, ufs_next_entry() returns the same pointer and the ufs_find_entry() loop does not reach its endpoint."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
