    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:02:58.988Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mt76: mt7996: unlink TWT flow if the MCU rejects the agreement\n\nmt7996_mac_add_twt_setup() links a new flow into dev->twt_list before\nasking the MCU to add the agreement, but exits without unlinking the flow\nif the request fails. The flow-ID and table masks are only committed\nafter MCU success, so the still-linked flow is invisible to the\nmask-gated teardown paths.\n\nIf the station retries the setup, the same slot is cleared and its\nembedded list node reinitialized while it is still linked, corrupting\ndev->twt_list. If the station disassociates instead, teardown skips the\nflow and station storage can be freed while the node remains linked. A\nlater scheduling or debugfs twt_stats walk can then dereference the\nfreed flow, resulting in a use-after-free.\n\nUnlink the flow when the MCU rejects the agreement. This restores the\nlist to its pre-request state while leaving the unchanged mask and\nagreement accounting untouched."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.5,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:A - A WiFi peer triggers mt7996_mac_add_twt_setup by sending a WLAN_S1G_TWT_SETUP action frame. ieee80211_rx_h_action queues it from ieee80211_process_rx_twt_action, and ieee80211_s1g_rx_twt_setup calls drv_add_twt_setup. The agreement parameters ride in that 802.11 action frame, so the sender must be in radio range of the AP.\nAC:H - The flow stays on dev->twt_list with flowid_mask clear only when mt7996_mcu_twt_agrt_update returns non-zero. Requests the driver already treats as unsupported (sta->mlo, min_twt_dur < MT7996_MIN_TWT_DUR, and mt7996_mac_check_twt_req) return before the list insert. The remaining failure is a non-zero UNI event->status or -ETIMEDOUT from mt7996_mcu_parse_response, and the tree shows no TWT field that produces that status.\nPR:N - ieee80211_process_rx_twt_action requires rx->sta but not WLAN_STA_AUTHORIZED. _ieee80211_is_robust_mgmt_frame excludes WLAN_CATEGORY_S1G, so ieee80211_drop_unencrypted_mgmt keeps the unprotected setup. Open-system authentication inserts the station before any PSK or 802.1X handshake.\nUI:N - The attacker completes open-system authentication, transmits the TWT Setup, and then either retries the setup or deauthenticates. The subsequent list walk is caused by that second setup frame.\nS:U - The dangling mt7996_twt_flow is embedded in the sta_info object from __sta_info_alloc and released by sta_info_free, while dev->twt_list is state of that same AP kernel.\nC:H - On disassociation mt7996_mac_twt_teardown_flow returns without list_del because the flowid_mask bit was never set, then sta_info_free kfree's the station. The next mt7996_mac_twt_sched_list_add walk reads that freed embedded flow, a heap use-after-free.\nI:H - mt7996_mac_twt_sched_list_add calls list_add through the freed flow's list pointers, and a retry memset's and INIT_LIST_HEAD's flow[flowid] while that node is still on dev->twt_list. Reusing the freed sta_info slot turns those stores into an attacker-controlled write.\nA:H - Retrying setup turns the still-linked flow into a self-loop, so mt7996_mac_twt_sched_list_add never finishes and holds dev->mt76.mutex. Disassociation instead leaves the node linked until sta_info_free, and the same walk or mt7996_twt_stats then dereferences freed memory."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
