    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:03:12.318Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmedia: imx-jpeg: cancel timeout worker when streaming stops\n\nEach per-fd context ctx owns a delayed_work (ctx->task_timer, callback\nmxc_jpeg_device_run_timeout) armed via schedule_delayed_work() at the end\nof mxc_jpeg_device_run() to recover a stalled encode/decode job. The only\nexisting cancellation is cancel_delayed_work() in the frame-done IRQ\nhandler, which de-queues a pending work item but does not wait for a\ncallback that has already started, and it only runs when a frame completes.\n\nWhen the fd is closed while a job is in flight (the frame-done IRQ has not\nfired yet), nothing syncs the worker before mxc_jpeg_release() frees ctx\nwith kfree() after v4l2_m2m_ctx_release(). A queued or executing\nmxc_jpeg_device_run_timeout() can then recover ctx through\ncontainer_of(&ctx->task_timer) and dereference it (ctx->mxc_jpeg,\nslot_data, dev_warn) after ctx has been freed.\n\nCancel the worker from mxc_jpeg_stop_streaming(). The cancel cannot live\nin mxc_jpeg_release(): mxc_jpeg_device_run() arms the timer while holding\nonly hw_lock, not the mxc_jpeg->lock mutex that release holds, so a cancel\nin release could still race a concurrent mxc_jpeg_device_run() that\nre-arms the timer afterwards. mxc_jpeg_stop_streaming() instead runs inside\nv4l2_m2m_ctx_release() -> vb2_queue_release(), i.e. after\nv4l2_m2m_cancel_job() has set TRANS_ABORT and waited for any in-flight job\nto finish (so __v4l2_m2m_try_queue() will not queue and v4l2_m2m_try_run()\nwill not run any further job for this context, which prevents\nmxc_jpeg_device_run() from re-arming the timer) and before the m2m context\nis freed. cancel_delayed_work_sync() removes a pending work item and waits\nfor a running callback, so the worker can no longer race with the\nsubsequent kfree(). The cancel is placed before the buffer-release loop so\na concurrently running timeout callback cannot race with it over the same\nbuffers. If the frame-done IRQ canceled a still-pending timer, this cancel\nis a no-op; if the timeout callback has already started, it waits for the\ncallback to finish. The same mxc_jpeg_stop_streaming() call is also\nreached from VIDIOC_STREAMOFF, which drains the worker early, although\nSTREAMOFF itself does not free ctx -- the use-after-free arises only\nwhen the fd is later closed.\n\nThis bug was found by static analysis."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - A local process opens the node registered in mxc_jpeg_probe() and, via VIDIOC_QBUF/STREAMON, v4l2_m2m_try_run() calls mxc_jpeg_device_run() to arm ctx->task_timer. close() reaches mxc_jpeg_release() -> v4l2_m2m_ctx_release() -> mxc_jpeg_stop_streaming() and then kfree(ctx); no remote protocol carries the trigger.\nAC:H - v4l2_m2m_ctx_release() waits in v4l2_m2m_cancel_job() until TRANS_RUNNING clears, and mxc_jpeg_dec_irq() cancel_delayed_work()s ctx->task_timer before v4l2_m2m_job_finish(). The UAF needs the 2000ms mxc_jpeg_device_run_timeout() callback to already be running and still touch ctx after that wait; a normal frame-done disarms a pending timer, and the attacker cannot force that overlap.\nPR:L - mxc_jpeg_open() and the streaming ioctls in mxc_jpeg_ioctl_ops (VIDIOC_QBUF, VIDIOC_STREAMON) never call capable(). Any local user who can open the /dev/video node can queue a job and close the fd; CAP_SYS_ADMIN is only checked for unrelated v4l2 debug-register ioctls.\nUI:N - The same process opens the node, queues a buffer, starts streaming so mxc_jpeg_device_run() arms the timer, and closes the fd. No other user has to mount media or take an action.\nS:U - The object freed by mxc_jpeg_release() is the host-kernel mxc_jpeg_ctx allocated in mxc_jpeg_open(). The timeout callback's later use of that ctx stays inside the same kernel security domain.\nC:H - mxc_jpeg_device_run_timeout() rebuilds ctx with container_of() on the embedded task_timer and reads ctx->mxc_jpeg and ctx->fh.m2m_ctx after kfree(). Those fields sit in the kmalloc slab, so reclaiming the object feeds attacker-controlled kernel pointers into the callback.\nI:H - On that freed ctx the callback spin_lock()s ctx->mxc_jpeg->hw_lock and, when slot_data.used is set, calls mxc_jpeg_job_finish() and v4l2_m2m_job_finish(). That writes kernel memory through the reclaimed mxc_jpeg and m2m_ctx pointers.\nA:H - The callback runs in kernel worker context. After kfree(ctx), its first dereference of ctx->mxc_jpeg and the hw_lock acquisition fault or oops the kernel, and mxc_jpeg_job_finish() will also walk freed m2m buffers."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
