{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99471",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.417Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:42:57.597Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:03:16.250Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:03:16.250Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: PPC: Book3S HV: Add SRCU protection for virtual-mode HPT hcalls\n\nThe virtual-mode HPT hcall handlers (H_ENTER, H_REMOVE, H_BULK_REMOVE,\nH_CLEAR_REF, H_CLEAR_MOD) call into book3s_hv_rm_mmu.c, which accesses\nmemslots via kvm_memslots_raw().  kvm_memslots_raw() uses\nrcu_dereference_raw_check() to bypass SRCU lockdep annotation checking.\nThis is safe in real mode because the entire guest entry/exit is wrapped\nin srcu_read_lock/unlock inside kvmppc_run_core() and\nkvmhv_run_single_vcpu().\n\nHowever, since commit 6165d5dd99db (\"KVM: PPC: Book3S HV: add virtual\nmode handlers for HPT hcalls and page faults\"), these same handlers are\nalso executed in virtual mode via kvmppc_pseries_do_hcall(), which runs\nafter SRCU has already been released on guest exit.\n\nA concurrent KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION deletion or move can therefore\ncall synchronize_srcu_expedited() — which does not wait for this thread —\nand then kfree(slot) and vfree(slot->arch.rmap) while the hcall handler\nstill holds a raw pointer to the memslot.  lock_rmap() then writes to\nfreed vmalloc memory, leading to use-after-free and memory corruption.\nBecause kvm_memslots_raw() suppresses lockdep checks, this race is\nentirely silent.\n\nFix this by factoring out the 7 HPT hcall handlers (H_REMOVE, H_ENTER,\nH_READ, H_CLEAR_MOD, H_CLEAR_REF, H_PROTECT, H_BULK_REMOVE) into a\nhelper function, kvmppc_pseries_do_hpt_hcall(), and wrapping its call\nsite in srcu_read_lock(&kvm->srcu) / srcu_read_unlock(&kvm->srcu, idx).\nTargeting only the HPT hcalls avoids wrapping non-HPT hcalls that sleep\n(such as H_CONFER, H_REGISTER_VPA, H_PAGE_INIT) or handlers that already\nacquire SRCU internally (such as H_RTAS)."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - A host user reaches this from KVM_RUN (kvm_arch_vcpu_ioctl_run → kvmppc_vcpu_run_hv): after kvmhv_run_single_vcpu drops kvm->srcu, a supervisor-mode H_ENTER is handled in kvmppc_pseries_do_hcall → kvmppc_h_enter → kvmppc_do_h_enter, which calls kvm_memslots_raw(). No network protocol carries that input.\nAC:L - That user owns the vm fd, so one thread loops guest H_ENTER while another issues KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION delete or move. kvm_set_memslot's synchronize_srcu_expedited does not wait for the hcall, then kvm_free_memslot frees the slot. The same user selects HPT mode with KVM_PPC_CONFIGURE_V3_MMU.\nPR:L - arch/powerpc/kvm has no capable() check. kvm_dev_ioctl(KVM_CREATE_VM) and KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION need only an open /dev/kvm fd and kvm->mm == current->mm. kvmppc_handle_exit_hv drops sc 1 when MSR_PR is set, and the VM owner runs the guest with MSR_PR clear.\nUI:N - The VM owner issues both the H_ENTER hypercall through KVM_RUN and the memslot delete or move. No other principal has to mount an image, hot-unplug memory, or start migration.\nS:U - kvm_vm_ioctl frees a slot only when kvm->mm == current->mm, so the freeing thread is the host VM owner. lock_rmap then corrupts host kernel memory on that owner's KVM_RUN thread, under the host kernel's own authority.\nC:H - After kvmppc_core_free_memslot_hv vfree's arch.rmap, kvmppc_do_h_enter still reads *rmap and kvmppc_add_revmap_chain consumes KVMPPC_RMAP_PRESENT and KVMPPC_RMAP_INDEX from it. That use-after-free of a reclaimed page is a host kernel read of freed data.\nI:H - lock_rmap's test_and_set_bit_lock and kvmppc_add_revmap_chain's store of the guest-chosen pte_index both write through the dangling rmap pointer. On a move, kvm_commit_memory_region kfree's the slot while do_h_enter still loads memslot->arch.rmap from it.\nA:H - Those stores target a page kvmppc_core_free_memslot_hv has returned to the allocator. Corrupting the freelist or a reused kernel object on the vcpu thread oopses or panics the host."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
                    "vendor": "Linux",
                    "defaultStatus": "unaffected",
