    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99477",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.417Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:43:11.195Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-22T11:08:33.584Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-22T11:08:33.584Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfs/proc/task_mmu: pin mm_users in do_pagemap_scan()\n\nThe PAGEMAP_SCAN ioctl takes mmap_read_lock() on an mm obtained from\n/proc/<pid>/pagemap without holding a reference to mm_users.\n\nproc_mem_open() deliberately grabs only mm_count, so a consumer that\ntouches the address space has to call mmget_not_zero() first.  Every other\nconsumer in this file does -- m_start(), do_procmap_query(),\nshow_smaps_rollup() and pagemap_read() -- but do_pagemap_scan() does not.\n\nWithout that reference the target can reach exit_mmap() concurrently, and\nexit_mmap() calls unmap_vmas() while holding mmap_lock only for reading,\nwhich does not exclude the scanner.  For ordinary VMAs this is harmless,\nas free_pgtables() and remove_vma() run afterwards under mmap_write_lock. \nA shared hugetlb VMA is different: __hugetlb_zap_end() frees the VMA's\nhugetlb_vma_lock inside the read-locked phase.\n\nwalk_hugetlb_range() can therefore load vma->vm_private_data, have the\nlock freed underneath it, and then down_read() a freed rw_semaphore.  The\nload happens before the freeing side clears the pointer, so this is a\nuse-after-free and not a NULL dereference.  Any unprivileged user can\nreach it with no huge pages configured, as hugetlb_reserve_pages()\nallocates the lock before the VM_NORESERVE early return.\n\nPin mm_users across the scan, the way the other consumers do.  mmput()\nfrom the exiting task can then no longer reach exit_mmap() while a walk is\nin progress.\n\n[akpm@linux-foundation.org: remove unneeded & undesirable test for !mm]"
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - PAGEMAP_SCAN is issued through do_pagemap_cmd() on a /proc/<pid>/pagemap fd opened by pagemap_open(). do_pagemap_scan() walks that mm without mmget_not_zero(), so the triggering action is a local ioctl racing the target task's exit_mmap().\nAC:L - The attacker mmap()s MAP_SHARED|MAP_HUGETLB|MAP_NORESERVE, so hugetlb_reserve_pages() calls hugetlb_vma_lock_alloc() and returns on VMA_NORESERVE with no hugepage pool, then overlaps walk_hugetlb_range() with that task's exit_mmap(). Both tasks are the attacker's.\nPR:L - /proc/<pid>/pagemap is mode S_IRUSR. pagemap_open() reaches proc_mem_open() with PTRACE_MODE_READ, which __ptrace_may_access() grants to the same uid, and Yama applies its ptrace_scope check only to PTRACE_MODE_ATTACH. do_pagemap_cmd() has no capability check.\nUI:N - The attacker creates the shared hugetlb VMA, opens that task's pagemap, issues PAGEMAP_SCAN, and exits the task. Exploitation is entirely the attacker's own syscalls.\nS:U - The use-after-free frees the scanned task's hugetlb_vma_lock in the kernel that runs do_pagemap_scan(). The corruption remains inside that kernel security authority.\nC:H - hugetlb_vma_lock_read() holds the vma->vm_private_data pointer and down_read()s it after __hugetlb_vma_unlock_write_put() has kref_put() that hugetlb_vma_lock into kfree() in hugetlb_vma_lock_release(). rwsem_down_read_slowpath() then loads sem->first_waiter from the reclaimed object and list_add_tail() reads through it.\nI:H - rwsem_read_trylock() atomic_long_add_return_acquire()s RWSEM_READER_BIAS into the freed rw_semaphore count, and rwsem_down_read_slowpath() list_add_tail()s a waiter through sem->first_waiter. Reclaiming the hugetlb_vma_lock turns those stores into a kernel write.\nA:H - down_read() on the kfree'd hugetlb_vma_lock rw_semaphore dereferences a poisoned or reused slab object, and the slow path can sleep uninterruptibly in rwsem_down_read_slowpath(). That oopses or hangs the kernel, and the attacker can retry against a new task."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
