    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:03:23.671Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nALSA: emux: Fix UAF of soundfont zones at note-on\n\nsnd_emux_note_on() looks up the matching zones via\nsnd_soundfont_search_zone(), which takes sflist->lock only during the\nlookup and returns the raw zone pointers after releasing it.  The\ncaller then stores them in vp->zone without any protection.\nMeanwhile, a concurrent SNDRV_EMUX_IOCTL_REMOVE_LAST_SAMPLES ioctl may\nunlink and free those zones in snd_soundfont_remove_unlocked(); this\nleads to a use-after-free in snd_emux_note_on(), and later at\naccessing the cached vp->zone, e.g. in snd_emux_send_effect().\n\nFix it by holding sflist->lock in snd_emux_note_on() over the whole\nzone lookup and voice setup, and let snd_soundfont_search_zone()\nrequire the caller to hold the lock, instead.\nsnd_soundfont_lock_preset() takes sflist->lock for setting\npresets_locked, so the removal path now syncs with the note-on finish."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The zone is freed by the local ioctl SNDRV_EMUX_IOCTL_REMOVE_LAST_SAMPLES in snd_emux_hwdep_ioctl(), which calls snd_soundfont_remove_unlocked(), racing a SNDRV_SEQ_EVENT_NOTEON from snd_seq_write() through snd_emux_event_input() and snd_midi_process_event() into snd_emux_note_on(). Both of those entry points are local device operations.\nAC:L - The same user holds the hwdep fd and a sequencer client, loads an unlocked font with SNDRV_EMUX_IOCTL_LOAD_PATCH, and runs note-on concurrently with REMOVE_LAST_SAMPLES. Before the fix, snd_soundfont_search_zone() drops sflist->lock before snd_emux_note_on() uses the returned zone pointers, and note-on does not take presets_mutex, so the attacker controls both sides and can retry.\nPR:L - snd_hwdep_open(), snd_seq_open(), and snd_emux_hwdep_ioctl() do not call capable(). The emux port is created with SNDRV_SEQ_PORT_CAP_WRITE|SNDRV_SEQ_PORT_CAP_SUBS_WRITE, so a local user who can open the Emux WaveTable hwdep node and /dev/snd/seq can issue REMOVE_LAST_SAMPLES and deliver the note-on.\nUI:N - The attacker opens the hwdep node and /dev/snd/seq, loads the soundfont with SNDRV_EMUX_IOCTL_LOAD_PATCH, writes SNDRV_SEQ_EVENT_NOTEON, and issues SNDRV_EMUX_IOCTL_REMOVE_LAST_SAMPLES. Every step is their own action on those file descriptors.\nS:U - The dangling snd_sf_zone is consumed in the host kernel by snd_emux_note_on() and by emu10k1 start_voice(). The corrupted object and the impact both sit in that kernel security authority.\nC:H - snd_emux_note_on() copies the freed zone's soundfont_voice_info into vp->reg and, when zone->sample is non-NULL, reads sample->block. Reclaiming the kzalloc'd snd_sf_zone lets the attacker choose that pointer, and emu10k1 start_voice() then reads fields of the resulting snd_emu10k1_memblk.\nI:H - emu10k1 start_voice() casts vp->block, taken from the freed zone's sample, to snd_emu10k1_memblk and increments emem->map_locked. snd_emu10k1_memblk_map() then list_move_tails mapped_order_link when mapped_page >= 0, so a reclaimed zone becomes a kernel write and list corruption.\nA:H - snd_emux_note_on() and snd_emux_send_effect() dereference the snd_sf_zone after snd_soundfont_remove_unlocked() has kfree'd it, and start_voice() increments map_locked on the block pointer taken from that zone. Those kernel-context accesses oops the machine."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
