{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99544",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.421Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-19T14:45:02.316Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:07.981Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:07.981Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nDrivers: hv: vmbus: Bounds check the shared ring buffer indices\n\nIn a CoCo VM the host is untrusted. Since the VMBus ring buffer read and\nwrite indices live in shared memory, the guest has to treat both as\npotentially malicious.\n\nhv_ringbuffer_write() copies into the ring at write_index with no bounds\nchecking, so the host can make the guest write packet data at any offset\nup to 4 GiB past the start of the ring buffer. read_index matters too:\nthe available space derived from both indices is the only bound on how\nmuch is copied, and an out-of-range index can make it far larger than\nthe ring.\n\nThe fix is to validate both indices before use and to use the validated\nsnapshot instead of re-accessing. For invalid indices,\nhv_ringbuffer_write() logs and returns -EIO.\n\nOne re-read of write_index is left unchecked in this patch:\nhv_get_ring_bufferindices() fetches it again for the prev_indices\ntrailer. The value is only returned to the host and is not used as an\noffset or length for indexing. Since this is not a security issue, it is\naddressed later in the series.\n\nOpen-coding the bytes available arithmetic keeps the fix free of\nprerequisites; a later patch refactors it into a helper function."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 9.3,
                        "baseSeverity": "CRITICAL"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The Hyper-V host writes hv_ring_buffer.read_index and write_index in the decrypted ring-header page that __vmbus_establish_gpadl() shares for a non-confidential channel. hv_ringbuffer_write() uses that write_index as the memcpy offset when heartbeat_onchannelcallback() replies through vmbus_sendpacket().\nAC:L - The host offers the heartbeat channel without VMBUS_CHANNEL_CONFIDENTIAL_RING_BUFFER so the ring stays shared, then sets both indices so hv_get_bytes_to_write() wraps past the size check. heartbeat_onchannelcallback() answers the host's own packet via vmbus_sendpacket(), so the copy is under the host's control.\nPR:N - The host writes the shared outbound ring header from outside the guest and presents no guest credential. vmbus_onoffer() accepts the heartbeat device because allowed_in_isolated is set, and the reply runs in kernel context.\nUI:N - The host posts the inbound heartbeat and poisons the outbound indices itself. heartbeat_onchannelcallback() sends the reply from the guest kernel as part of handling that packet.\nS:C - hv_copyto_ringbuffer() memcpy's at the ring data pointer plus the host-chosen write_index, past the double-mapped vmap, so bytes that should stay in the host-shared ring land in other guest kernel memory across the CoCo private boundary.\nC:H - The echoed hbeat_txf_buf, whose bytes the host chose and whose length is recvlen up to one page, is written at a host-chosen u32 offset. That corrupts guest pointers later VMBus replies use to copy private memory into the shared ring.\nI:H - hv_copyto_ringbuffer() memcpy's the outbound packet at write_index with no upper bound against ring_datasize, a host-chosen offset up to 4GiB past the ring, using bytes from the host-supplied heartbeat buffer.\nA:H - The memcpy in hv_copyto_ringbuffer() runs while hv_ringbuffer_write() holds outring_info->ring_lock with IRQs disabled, so a write_index on the vmap guard page or an unmapped hole faults and oopses the guest."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
