    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99574",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.422Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:49:07.960Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:32.968Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:32.968Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nf2fs: bound the SIT/NAT bitmaps by the checkpoint pack\n\nsit_ver_bitmap_bytesize and nat_ver_bitmap_bytesize are used as memcpy\nlengths into and out of sbi->ckpt, which is sized from cp_payload alone,\nand nothing compares either length against that allocation.\n\nsanity_check_ckpt() has checked these two fields since c77ec61ca0a4, which\nadded them after a crafted image overran build_sit_info(). That check ties\neach length to a segment count; it never compares either against the\nallocation, which is the half this adds.\n\nMounting over-reads in build_sit_info() and build_node_manager(), and the\nfirst checkpoint writes the same lengths back through the same pointers,\nso the over-read's contents reach the on-disk checkpoint. An unmount is\nenough; the filesystem does not have to be used.\n\nBound both ends the way __bitmap_ptr() lays them out. The three branches\ndiffer, so a single sum of the two lengths would still admit a small\noverflow when cp_payload is non-zero."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - sit_ver_bitmap_bytesize and nat_ver_bitmap_bytesize are taken from the on-disk checkpoint in f2fs_get_valid_checkpoint() and passed to kmemdup() in build_sit_info() and init_node_manager(); those bytes are image metadata, not a field of any network protocol message.\nAC:L - The attacker picks segment_count_sit and segment_count_nat so f2fs_sanity_check_ckpt()'s segment-count test accepts the lengths, while cp_payload leaves the sbi->ckpt kvzalloc shorter than __bitmap_ptr() plus that length; the mount-time kmemdup then runs with no race or hardware fault.\nPR:N - The copy runs in f2fs_fill_super() as soon as the image is mounted. f2fs_fs_type has no FS_USERNS_MOUNT, so mount_capable() requires init-namespace CAP_SYS_ADMIN, but that capability belongs to the mounting victim; the image supplier needs none.\nUI:R - build_sit_info() is reached only from f2fs_fill_super() through f2fs_get_tree() and get_tree_bdev(), which run when someone mounts this image. A well-formed, already-mounted f2fs never reloads these bitmap lengths.\nS:U - The kmemdup past sbi->ckpt and the memcpy in get_sit_bitmap() and get_nat_bitmap() corrupt host kernel heap only. Nothing here crosses a guest-to-host or IOMMU boundary.\nC:H - build_sit_info() and init_node_manager() kmemdup() sit_ver_bitmap_bytesize and nat_ver_bitmap_bytesize bytes from __bitmap_ptr() inside an sbi->ckpt of (1+cp_payload) blocks. The excess is an attacker-sized read of adjacent kernel memory, not a few-byte leak.\nI:H - do_checkpoint() calls get_sit_bitmap() and get_nat_bitmap(), which memcpy those same lengths back through __bitmap_ptr(). The tail past the kvzalloc of sbi->ckpt is a heap out-of-bounds write, reached from kill_f2fs_super() when CP_UMOUNT_FLAG is clear.\nA:H - The memcpy in get_sit_bitmap() and get_nat_bitmap() writes past sbi->ckpt into adjacent heap, and a sit/nat length that runs off the direct map faults inside the mount-time kmemdup. Either way the kernel oopses."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
