{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99582",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.423Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:49:13.519Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:38.552Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:38.552Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsctp: re-point retained control chunks on association migration\n\nsctp_sock_migrate() transfers DATA chunk ownership but leaves retained\ncontrol chunks pointing at the old socket. A later retransmission can\ntherefore access the socket after it has been freed.\n\nExtend the migration walk to cover retained control chunks and use\nsctp_control_set_owner_w() to assign their new owner. Reuse the DATA\nownership check so each chunk is processed once per pass, even when it\nis reachable through multiple lists or retained pointers.\n\nPreserve a control chunk's existing authentication key across migration\nso its final release can still generate SCTP_AUTH_FREE_KEY when needed."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - sctp_sock_migrate() is only reached from sctp_accept() and from getsockopt(SCTP_SOCKOPT_PEELOFF) via sctp_do_peeloff()/sctp_clone_sock(). It leaves retained control chunks (strreset_chunk, addip_last_asconf, asconf_ack_list) with skb->sk still naming the old socket, which is freed by a later local close(); a remote SCTP peer cannot call peeloff/accept or free that sock.\nAC:L - The attacker sets SCTP_RECONFIG_SUPPORTED and SCTP_ENABLE_STREAM_RESET, handshakes with a peer it controls that advertises the RECONF extension and then withholds the response, and calls SCTP_RESET_STREAMS so sctp_send_reset_streams() holds asoc->strreset_chunk. Peeloff and close() free the old sock before the reconf_timer runs sctp_generate_reconf_event() -> sctp_sf_send_reconf().\nPR:L - socket(SOCK_SEQPACKET, IPPROTO_SCTP) plus SCTP_RECONFIG_SUPPORTED, SCTP_ENABLE_STREAM_RESET, SCTP_RESET_STREAMS, and SCTP_SOCKOPT_PEELOFF perform no capability check. The only credential gate in net/sctp/socket.c on this path is CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for ports below 1024, which an ephemeral outbound connect does not need.\nUI:N - The same local user creates the socket, enables RE-CONFIG, peels the association, and closes the old fd. sctp_generate_reconf_event() then runs from the transport reconf_timer; no other user must accept, mount, or confirm.\nS:U - The stale pointer is the caller's own struct sock. sctp_packet_transmit() corrupts that kernel object in the same security domain; the path does not cross a guest/host or IOMMU boundary.\nC:H - Retransmission loads the freed sock from chunk->skb->sk. skb_set_owner_w() reads sk_fullsock()/sk_wmem_alloc and sctp_v4_xmit() reads inet_sk(sk)->tos and passes sk to __ip_queue_xmit(). The RTO delay after close() is enough to reclaim the sock slab.\nI:H - skb_set_owner_w() in sctp_packet_transmit() __refcount_add()s the freed sk->sk_wmem_alloc, sctp_v4_ecn_capable() writes inet_sk(sk)->tos, and sctp_v4_xmit() stores inet->pmtudisc. Reclaiming the object before sctp_sf_send_reconf() resends strreset_chunk makes that a write primitive.\nA:H - Even without slab reuse, sctp_packet_transmit() dereferences the freed sock in skb_set_owner_w() and the later sock_wfree() on the packet skb, which oopses the kernel when the reconf timer retransmits strreset_chunk."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
