                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:04:44.000Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nceph: clamp the inline data length in ceph_fill_inline_data()\n\nThe MDS decides how much inline data to attach to a reply, and the\nclient parses inline_len without any upper bound: ceph_decode_need()\nonly verifies that the message actually carries that many bytes.\nceph_fill_inline_data() then copies the data into a single folio\nwith no length check, so a malicious or buggy MDS returning more\ninline data than the folio can hold writes past its end.\n\nClamp the length to folio_size() so both callers, handle_cap_grant()\nand fill_inode(), are covered."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 9.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "CRITICAL"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - The MDS supplies inline_len and the following payload in a CEPH_MSG_CLIENT_REPLY, decoded by parse_reply_info_inline_data and passed through handle_reply and ceph_fill_inode, or in a v4+ CEPH_MSG_CLIENT_CAPS decoded by parse_inline_data and passed through ceph_handle_caps and handle_cap_grant; both copy it in ceph_fill_inline_data over the TCP messenger.\nAC:L - A new inode has i_inline_version 0 and i_truncate_seq 0. The MDS reply sets a non-directory mode, size > 0, inline_version greater than 1, and CEPH_CAP_FILE_CACHE, so ceph_fill_file_size stores a non-zero i_size and ceph_fill_inode copies into a freshly created, non-uptodate page 0.\nPR:N - handle_reply and ceph_handle_caps accept inline_len from the MDS peer on the session opened by ceph_real_mount. That peer is the remote cluster endpoint, and the copy runs with no local user credential consulted.\nUI:N - open_root_dentry issues CEPH_MDS_OP_GETATTR during ceph_real_mount, and that reply is what handle_reply feeds to ceph_fill_inode. The MDS delivers inline_len in its own response; no later user action is required.\nS:U - The memcpy corrupts memory of the kernel that mounted ceph, which is the same security authority as the filesystem client.\nC:H - ceph_fill_inline_data memcpy's MDS-chosen bytes from the message through kmap_atomic or memcpy_to_folio past the single page at index 0, so the write continues into the adjacent direct-map page and can corrupt neighboring kernel objects into a disclosure primitive.\nI:H - inline_len is the u32 from the MDS message, checked only by ceph_decode_need against the message end. The destination page is PAGE_SIZE, and the memcpy writes that full attacker-chosen length past the end of the page.\nA:H - The overrun past that page writes into adjacent kernel memory and oopses the client; inline_len can extend to the messenger front limit of 16MB."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
