                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - udf_table_new_block() mis-handles the type field of an on-disk unallocated-space-table extent. It is reached when udf_create() -> udf_new_inode() or udf_file_write_iter() -> udf_write_begin() -> inode_getblk() -> udf_add_aext() calls udf_new_block() on a mounted UDF volume; that extent is not a field of any remote protocol message.\nAC:L - The attacker builds the image with an unallocated space table whose free extents are type 1 (EXT_NOT_RECORDED_ALLOCATED), which mkudffs --space=unalloctable writes. Taking the last block in udf_table_new_block() then always leaves goal_elen non-zero because the type bits survive the subtraction, and the next allocation expands that empty extent. No race or external failure is required.\nPR:N - udf_fstype.fs_flags is only FS_REQUIRES_DEV, so mount_capable() demands capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN) and a user namespace cannot mount UDF. The attacker never needs that capability: the image is supplied to a victim, and the victim's create or pwrite is what enters udf_table_new_block().\nUI:R - udf_fill_super() only loads the unallocated space table; it does not allocate. The victim must mount the image and create or write a file so udf_new_block() consumes the last block of a type-1 extent. Mount alone does not trip the bug.\nS:U - udf_get_pblock() turns the bogus logical blocks into sectors of the same block device that was mounted, which is where the reserve volume descriptor sequence and backup anchor live. The damage does not cross a guest/host or other security boundary.\nC:N - __block_write_begin_int() does not bh_read a buffer_new block: it zeroes the unwritten edges and the write replaces the middle. udf_bread() memsets UDF_BLK_NEW buffers, and udf_write_inode() memsets the file-entry block. The alias overwrites prior blocks; it does not return them to userspace.\nI:H - After the last block of a type-1 extent, udf_table_new_block() stores a length-zero extent and the next goal_elen subtraction from 0x40000000 records a type-0 run of 2^30-blocksize bytes. Following udf_new_inode() and udf_file_write_iter() writes then cover in-use blocks, other file entries, and the reserve volume descriptor sequence.\nA:H - Creating files until that extent is exhausted writes file entries over the reserve volume descriptor sequence and the backup anchor, so the volume is no longer usable. The same alias makes udf_file_write_iter() WARN in __udf_add_aext() after udf_setup_indirect_aext() zeroes the allocation-extent block it is filling, and udf_do_extend_file() WARN_ONs in udf_truncate_extents()."
                        }
                    ]
                }
